  "body": "From the review of PR #135 (#103): https://github.com/NeoTech/Camcraft/pull/135#issuecomment-6713305279 (\"Body clearance during the ride\", and the earlier \"the body is judged on axial rapids only\").\n\nAfter #103, the turned family's rapid law judges a boring bar's whole body on its AXIAL RAPIDS: the head toward the axis from the insert, and a shank wider than the head reaching past the nose. The cutting FEEDS are judged only by the nose's envelope verdict. The bar's head and shank in the hole during the ride, the plunge and the radial lead-out are not checked against the stock and the pilot ahead.\n\nCase: BR-1000 (Ø10 body, nose 0.4) boring a Ø12 hole over a Ø11 pilot. The ride's nose centre runs at r ≤ 5.6, so the body spans r ≈ -4.4..5.6, which stays inside the wall. But the band between the pilot (5.5) and the finished wall (6) is uncut stock until the ride passes. A bar whose head or shank stands outside the nose's swept kerf, ahead of or above the cut, is not judged. Neither is a stepped shank (shank_d > diameter) whose outward reach passes the nose during the ride. The app's `turning_bore` B4 fixture already raises `ShankHit` under the ride, so some of this is seen elsewhere, but not against the stock or the pilot.\n\nDone means: a bore's feeds are judged with the bar's body (`turning::bar_spans` over flute and shank) against the revolved stock, less the program ahead and this path's own earlier cuts, blocking the post on a hit. Pin it with a bar whose body cannot follow its own ride through the uncut band.\n",
